menu "AR Kart Firmware"

    choice ARKART_DRIVE_BACKEND
        prompt "Drive backend"
        default ARKART_DRIVE_BACKEND_PWM
        help
            How this kart's motors are driven. One firmware tree, one backend per build —
            the two karts can be built differently because the teardown found two different
            receiver boards in the same Tornado chassis.

        config ARKART_DRIVE_BACKEND_PWM
            bool "Plan M — PWM through kit transistors (drive.c)"
            help
                The stock receiver board is discarded. GPIO47 PWMs a low-side 2N2222 for the
                rear drive motor and GPIO41/42 drive the front steer coil through an H-bridge
                built from kit parts, all on LEDC timer 1. Requires the soldering in the
                build sheet and the five bench probes in motor_map.h.

        config ARKART_DRIVE_BACKEND_RXINJECT
            bool "Plan RX — radio-socket injection into the stock board (rx_inject.c)"
            help
                The stock single-chip receiver-decoder board (SA1607RX 330, SOP-16, an
                RX-2B-family five-function clone) stays in the car on its own batteries and
                keeps doing all the motor driving. Its plug-in radio module is removed and the
                ESP32 impersonates it, bit-banging the demodulated pulse-train into the
                decoder's data input (pin 14) with the RMT peripheral.
                Two wires total: ARKART_PIN_RX_DATA -> pin 14 / the socket's DATA pad,
                ESP32 GND -> the board's V- hole.
                No transistors; GPIO41/42 and LEDC timer 1 are unused.
                The pulse-count ladder is the RX-2B reference table, not a measured one —
                run the serial `sweep` before trusting it.
    endchoice

    config ARKART_RXINJECT_CONSOLE
        bool "Plan RX: serial calibration console"
        depends on ARKART_DRIVE_BACKEND_RXINJECT
        default y
        help
            A line parser on the console UART with sniff / sweep / code / set / fwd / steer /
            stop. This is how the pulse-count ladder gets calibrated against the actual clone
            in the car: `sniff` releases the pin to a high-impedance input and reads the real
            protocol off the wire while the car's own 27 MHz remote is pressed; `sweep`
            transmits candidate counts when no remote is available. Leave it on — it costs a
            few KB and a low-priority task, and it is the only way to retune the protocol
            without a reflash. Turn it off only for a race build where nothing may be typed
            at the kart.

    config ARKART_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID"
        default "arkart"
        help
            SSID the kart joins. Use your own router / travel AP on a clean channel —
            venue WiFi at a hackathon is the single most reliable way to lose a demo.

    config ARKART_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password"
        default "arkartarkart"
        help
            WPA2 passphrase. Leave empty for an open AP.

    config ARKART_KART_ID
        int "Kart ID"
        range 0 255
        default 1
        help
            This kart's id. The UDP control packet carries a kart_id in byte 2 and packets
            addressed to a different kart are dropped, so both karts can share a broadcast
            or the same subnet safely. Flash kart 1 and kart 2 with different values here —
            it is the ONLY per-kart difference in the whole firmware.

    config ARKART_UDP_PORT
        int "UDP control port"
        range 1 65535
        default 7777
        help
            Port the car bridge sends control packets to. The bridge hard-codes 7777;
            change both or neither.

    config ARKART_STDB_HOST
        string "SpacetimeDB host (IR tag hit reporting)"
        default "192.168.4.1"
        help
            Host or IP running `spacetime start`. The IR tag layer POSTs
            report_hit(shooter, victim) to http://<host>:<port>/v1/database/<db>/call/report_hit
            whenever this kart is tagged — the same HTTP reducer convention the vision bridge
            uses for report_gate. This is the ONLY thing in the firmware that talks to
            SpacetimeDB; drive control still comes from the car bridge over UDP.
            Put the laptop's address on the kart's own network here.

    config ARKART_STDB_PORT
        int "SpacetimeDB HTTP port"
        range 1 65535
        default 3000
        help
            `spacetime start` listens on 3000 by default.

    config ARKART_STDB_DB
        string "SpacetimeDB database name"
        default "arkart"
        help
            The published module's name, i.e. the `arkart` in
            `spacetime publish arkart`.

    config ARKART_USE_STATIC_IP
        bool "Use a static IP instead of DHCP"
        default n
        help
            The car bridge and the vision bridge both need to know each kart's address.
            A static IP removes the "which address did it get this time" step at the cost
            of having to pick addresses that fit your AP's subnet.

    config ARKART_STATIC_IP
        string "Static IP address"
        depends on ARKART_USE_STATIC_IP
        default "192.168.4.11"

    config ARKART_STATIC_GW
        string "Static gateway"
        depends on ARKART_USE_STATIC_IP
        default "192.168.4.1"

    config ARKART_STATIC_NETMASK
        string "Static netmask"
        depends on ARKART_USE_STATIC_IP
        default "255.255.255.0"

endmenu
